A||||8||Standard||Oversteering advice||||1||
Z||000000||Standard||10-23-2000||04:21 AM||||Had some nice rain up here tonight, and as I was going down a cloverleaf at a little under 30mph when the rear end broke loose and I got sideways... I was ready for it, I tend to drive a bit more careful with pretty worn 225's on the back, so I just slammed the wheel to the left and after sliding for a while the rear end bit and swung back around.  There wasn't anybody behind me but it was enough to get the heart pumping..<P>My question is, was there something I could have done to get out of the slide quicker?  I didn't touch the brakes, and the clutch was in when it started sliding, and I didn't brake or let the clutch out while I was sliding.  <P>One good thing about the rain.. I was able to keep up with a Saleen to about 60mph  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I didn't even get into the throttle until halfway through first gear, but everytime the guy in the Saleen shifted he spun the tires... pull away, shift, spin, repeat  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Pretty loud car, too.  I know I wouldn't have stood a chance if the road was dry!<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||10-23-2000||11:30 AM||||I did the same thing just a couple weeks ago with mine.  Had recently rained, road was still wet, and I was being a bit stupid.  Tried to make a dry 35 mph left corner at about 45 mph wet.  The rear kicked out, and I wound up making the left hand turn, turning the wheel right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I'm not Michael Shumacher, but in this situation you never want to stab the brakes.  Most people panic, smack the brakes, lock the fronts causing the rear to swing around or do 360's into the ditch.  I love watching drivers do this every winter up here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>It's mainly a balance with brakes (very little if any), and staying out of the gas.  Keeping the car in gear helps to slow down the rear wheels, and from the way it sounds, it helped your car to eventually snap back in-line.  <P>In my corner, I used a little brake, fought the rear from kicking around, but in doing so I had slowed the car some.  I released the brake and mine snapped back in-line pretty quick.  That was great but I needed to keep slowing because I was coming up to parked cars at a stop light  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> So I braked again, kicking the rear back out and balanced the amount of oversteer with braking till I got things back in-line again.  Luckily I made it and gave everyone around me a good show, well sort of.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Main thing is to stay calm.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  I know that's pretty tough to do.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">           <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||10-23-2000||02:17 PM||||FWIW, it seems my 1987 GT is more forgiving than my 1986 SE, as far as "bringing it back" in an oversteer situation.  Whether it's the suspension, tires, or whatever, I don't know, but maybe it's a combination of things.<P>Letting off the throttle in the 1986 SE in the middle of a corner seems the best way to cause it to go practically ballistic (Hmmm-! Wonder where we'll end up <B>this</B> time?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> ) Gently applying a little throttle brings it back - as long as it hasn't gone too far already  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !  Agree that panicking is the worst thing to do. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||elfiero||10-23-2000||02:59 PM||||Hey standard,you want to practice your over- <BR>-steer abilitys, come over and we can take<BR>my V8 out for a spin!||||reg||6
Z||000004||1FST2M6||10-23-2000||04:01 PM||||i know minnie is 4 hrs from Menahga.. where is Golden Valley? I'll be in Menahga/Sebeka area for hoho time... a week+... humm.. WINTER picture meet... Do you guys drive your fieros in the STOOPID deep snow? Last year there was 14" in Menahga and snowed the entire time i was there? they seem to run ok in the -14* weather? Great for Doughnuts! and even better for Standards test piloting around off ramps   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 10-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||10-23-2000||04:09 PM||||Just tell me when and where  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Stu, That's called drop throttle oversteer.  Normal behavior for a stock Fiero.  It could be that some little difference in the alignment setting's makes the SE a little more twitchy than the GT.  I know the alingment on my GT is far from perfect, but it still drives straight so I'm not going to worry about it until I replace the struts and I need to get it aligned.<P>Tigger, I know what you mean about winter driving.. it seems that people lose 30 i.q. points at the mere sight of a snowflake  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  Actually, people were driving pretty stupid last night and it was only rain.. even saw some poor sod in a truck who had rear-ended a cop car!  <P>I think I need to go find a big parking lot to go slide around in now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||10-23-2000||04:16 PM||||Golden Valley is about 5 minutes northwest of downtown minneapolis.  <P>I <B>love</B> driving my 2m4 in stoopid deep snow!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Drove it through a blizzard last year, it was pretty much a sled with wheels, since there was about 10-12" of unplowed wet heavy snow on the ground.. Got to hear the 'clunk' of snow chunks on the floorboards the entire way home.  Best thing about the -14* weather is that there isn't much worry about overheating  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  although after I first got my GT the antifreeze froze solid one night after work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> luckily it wasn't too far of a drive home.||||reg||1
Z||000007||zeusrekning||10-23-2000||04:23 PM||||I did the same thing a 6+ months ago. Going down I26 and it was raining. Suddenly i just seen the taillights of my g/f's brother(who was in front of me) out the pass. side window.But you know, no one stopped. my headlights were shineing in the trees towards on coming traffic on the interstate. A lady getting off  the on ramp seen me and stopped. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||frontal lobe||10-24-2000||12:39 AM||||I just about swapped ends today!  It had been raining and the pavement was wet.  I was taking a right turn on a right turn lane (good idea, I know) at about 20mph.  Traffic was just going to come so about 2/3 of the way through the turn I started to get on it a little bit--not even that hard.  Oh man, the driver's rear started to catch up to me quick!  I got off the gas right quick and steered and it straightened out.  Phew.  If I had been goofing off, that's one thing.||||reg||1
